subject_definitions:\n<Picture 1> is a survey photograph of the great kitchen: a wide stone hearth under an oak beam with a low fire lit, a long scrubbed oak table, copper pans on hooks, one candle. Only its architecture and lighting are taken from <Picture 1>; its emptiness stays in the photograph.\n\n<Subject 1> is Tansy, established in <Picture 2>: a small stout dormouse, round-faced with bright dark eyes and a flour smudge on one cheek, warm russet fur, a bun of whiskers tied with twine, a pink nose and paws faintly stained with berry juice. She wears rolled cream linen sleeves under a long ochre apron over a brown dress, with wooden clogs. The apron and stained paws are visible in every frame she appears in.\n\nsummary:\n[reference generation] ONE LOCKED-OFF MEDIUM SHOT in the great kitchen. Tansy works at the table by the hearth, alone. The camera is locked at a medium shot and does not move for the whole clip.\n\nretention_analysis:\nThe camera is locked at a medium shot and does not move for the whole clip. <Picture 1>: architecture and hearth-light fully_preserved; the kitchen is empty of anyone but Tansy from the first frame to the last. THE FIRST FRAME IS ALREADY a medium shot of Tansy standing at the oak table in the hearth light, the stone hearth and hanging pans behind her exactly as <Picture 1> shows them; it is not a wide, it is her. <Subject 1>: fully_preserved, apron and stained paws visible throughout.\n\ndetailed_description:\nIllustrated storybook realism on warm apricot-toned paper, visible paper grain and fibre, ink linework with watercolour and gouache washes, fur drawn as strokes never photographic, like a hand-painted picture-book brought to motion. [Shot 1 - the only shot] THE VIDEO OPENS on Tansy at the table, the hearth fire lit behind her. The camera is locked and does not move. She cuts a heel from a loaf of bread, wraps it in a cloth, and pauses, listening toward the gate. On the final frame she is still, cloth-wrapped bread in her paws; the picture holds to the last frame.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nRain, distant birdsong, the creak of old wood.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nA single low flute line, sparse, unresolved.
